../items/lem-common-domain-commutator-integrates-to-weyl-relations.md:79:3.1 Fix $s$ and consider $K(t)=U_a(s)U_b(t)U_a(-s)$ and $L(t)=e^{-ic_{ab}st}U_b(t)$. Both are strongly continuous unitary groups by their formulas and [F1]. For $K$, taking difference quotients after unitary conjugation shows its exact derivative domain is $U_a(s)D(A_b)$, with derivative $iB$. For $L$, differentiating the scalar factor shows that every vector of $D(A_b)$ has derivative $i(A_b-c_{ab}sI)$. Conversely, if the derivative of $L(t)x$ at zero exists, writing $U_b(t)x=e^{ic_{ab}st}L(t)x$ proves the derivative of $U_b(t)x$ exists, so $x\in D(A_b)$ by [F1]. Thus the exact generators agree by step 2.1; [F2] gives $K=L$. Multiplying on the right by $U_a(s)$ proves the claimed pairwise Weyl relation on all of $H$. This is the functional-calculus exponentiation step; no power series of unbounded operators was used. [given, F1, F2, step 2.1, algebra]
../items/lem-earring-holonomy-perturbations-regularize-all-trajectory-indices.md:55:3.1 **A genuine allowed parameter space preserving the critical jets.** Two inequivalent smooth connections are separated by a finite common-base holonomy tuple. Otherwise compactness of the conjugating unitary group gives one simultaneous conjugator for all tuples; the path-transport construction in [F2] would then give a global determinant-one gauge. Thus a chosen noncritical point on an embedded trajectory arc can be separated, by finitely many tuples, from all critical points and from the compact remainder of that path outside a slightly larger arc. In the finite tuple's smooth conjugation quotient, choose a bump supported in the separating open set. Products of this bump with smooth tuple functions give cylinder functions vanishing on neighborhoods of every critical point. Choose countably many such data dense on all separating tuple charts, and choose weighted $\ell^1$ constants as in [F2]. Their sum space $\mathcal R$ is separable, its functions and every derivative vanish at each critical point, and all the preceding field bounds hold. There need not be one common neighborhood on which every infinite sum vanishes; flat critical jets are the exact condition used. Uniform derivative bounds in fixed critical slices preserve invertibility and uniqueness of their zeros for sufficiently small $\rho\in\mathcal R$. Properness of the critical projection in [F2] excludes any new critical points outside those slices for a sequence $\rho\to0$. Hence these small perturbations retain exactly the stipulated critical points and Hessians. [F1, F2, F3, step 2.1, construct, algebra]
