../items/lem-steinhaus-and-pettis-for-second-countable-locally-compact-groups.md:87:1.4 The unitary group $U(K)$ of a separable Hilbert space is second countable in the strong operator topology: fix a finite or countable orthonormal basis $(e_n)$ padded to a sequence by [F7] and consider $\Psi:U(K)\to K^{\mathbb N}$, $\Psi(u)=(ue_n)_n$. It is injective (a unitary vanishing on a complete orthonormal system is zero) and continuous for the SOT by [F6]; conversely, if $u_i\to u$ in the initial topology of the coordinate maps $u\mapsto ue_n$, then for $\xi=\sum_nc_ne_n\in K$ and $\varepsilon>0$ choose $N$ with $\sum_{n>N}|c_n|^2<\varepsilon^2/16$; then $\|(u_i-u)\xi\|\le\sum_{n\le N}|c_n|\,\|(u_i-u)e_n\|+2\bigl(\sum_{n>N}|c_n|^2\bigr)^{1/2}<\varepsilon/2+\varepsilon/2$ for all $i$ beyond a suitable index, so $u_i\to u$ strongly. Thus the SOT on $U(K)$ is the initial topology of the countable family $(u\mapsto ue_n)_n$, making it homeomorphic to a subspace of the second-countable space $K^{\mathbb N}$, hence second countable. [F6, F7]
