../items/def-qc-ft-gadget-and-proper-code-contract.md:35:A proper fault-tolerant code contract consists of a finite-dimensional code subspace $Q$ in a fixed block of $m$ physical coordinates, a finite logical gate alphabet $G$, and a fixed finite family of encoder, initialization, error-correction, decoder/readout, gate, syndrome-measurement, reset, and idle gadgets. Throughout this page, **universal** means that the subgroup generated by $G$ and its adjoints is dense in the unitary group on the logical space, in the operator norm of [[def-operator-norm]] (the supremum over the unit ball). The parameter $d$ is an unlocated-error correction capacity: for an encoding isometry $V:H_L\to H_P$ and the declared decoded recovery channel $\mathcal R$, every channel $\mathcal N$ with Kraus operators supported on at most $d$ physical coordinates satisfies $\mathcal R\circ\mathcal N\circ\operatorname{Ad}_V=\operatorname{id}_{\mathcal B(H_L)}$, including after tensoring the identity on any finite reference. The support may differ between Kraus operators and is not supplied to the fixed recovery. This capacity is distinct from the stabilizer distance $d(S)$ of [[def-qc-stabilizer-distance]], the minimum weight of a Pauli centralizer class outside scalar phases times the stabilizer; a distance argument certifies capacity $d$ only after separately verifying $2d<d(S)$ and the relevant error-family hypotheses. When the concatenated-threshold theorem uses $m\ge 2d+1$, this inequality is an explicit hypothesis.
