+---
+id: def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data
+kind: definition
+title: Finite non-Abelian lattice gauge data
+status: draft
+origin: session
+dependency_level: 1
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+category: quantum-field-theory-mathematics
+axiom_base: "ZF + AC_omega; inherited only through the supplied Lie(G) construction in the non-Abelian gauge datum."
+deps:
+  - def-countable-choice
+  - def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data
+dependency_roles:
+  def-countable-choice: mathematical-premise
+  def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data: mathematical-premise
+justified_by:
+  - lem-qftx-lattice-data-well-defined
+provenance:
+  statement: ai-altered
+  proof: not-applicable
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "H. B. Meyer, Lattice QCD: A Brief Introduction, in Lattice QCD for Nuclear Physics, Lecture Notes in Physics 889"
+      url: "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73862/1/82.pdf.pdf"
+    - title: "Robert Lohmayer, Eigenvalue Distributions of Wilson Loops"
+      url: "https://epub.uni-regensburg.de/16031/1/EigenvalueDistributionsOfWilsonLoops.pdf"
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Definition
+
+Assume the axiom of countable choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$. Fix the compact noncommutative finite-dimensional real Lie group $G$, its Lie algebra $\mathfrak g=T_eG$, and a specified smooth finite-dimensional complex unitary representation $\rho:G\to U(W,h)$ on a Hermitian vector space $(W,h)$ supplied by a non-Abelian gauge datum. Thus the group multiplication and inversion are smooth, $G$ is compact in its manifold topology, and $\rho(g)$ preserves $h$ for every $g\in G$. No connection, principal bundle sector, action functional, or continuum theory is part of the lattice data below.
+
+A **finite oriented lattice two-complex** is a finite set $\mathcal V$ of vertices, a finite set $\mathcal E^+$ containing one chosen orientation of each edge, an endpoint map $s,t:\mathcal E^+\to\mathcal V$, and a finite set $\mathcal F$ of faces. Each face $p\in\mathcal F$ is supplied with a based closed edge word $(e_1,\ldots,e_k)$ in the oriented edges $\mathcal E^+\sqcup\overline{\mathcal E^+}$, where the endpoint of each edge is the starting point of the next and the last endpoint is the first starting point. For the reverse orientation $\bar e$ of $e\in\mathcal E^+$, set $s(\bar e)=t(e)$ and $t(\bar e)=s(e)$.
+
+A **link configuration** is a tuple $U=(U_e)_{e\in\mathcal E^+}\in G^{\mathcal E^+}$, with $U_{\bar e}:=U_e^{-1}$. The **holonomy of a face** $p=(e_1,\ldots,e_k)$ is the ordered product $U_p:=U_{e_1}\cdots U_{e_k}$ in traversal order, with the first traversed edge on the left. The vertex gauge group is $G^{\mathcal V}$ and acts by
+
+$$
+(h\cdot U)_e=h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1},\qquad h=(h_v)_{v\in\mathcal V}\in G^{\mathcal V}.
+$$
+
+Choose a normalized bi-invariant Borel probability measure $\mu_G$ on $G$. The link measure is the finite product $\mu_G^{\mathcal E^+}$. For each face choose a continuous function $\Phi_p:G\to\mathbb R$ satisfying $\Phi_p(gzg^{-1})=\Phi_p(z)$ and $\Phi_p(z^{-1})=\Phi_p(z)$. Define the dimensionless lattice action and its normalized expectation by
+
+$$
+S_\Lambda(U):=\sum_{p\in\mathcal F}\Phi_p(U_p),\qquad
+Z_\Lambda:=\int_{G^{\mathcal E^+}}e^{-S_\Lambda(U)}\,d\mu_G^{\mathcal E^+}(U),\qquad
+\langle O\rangle_\Lambda:=Z_\Lambda^{-1}\int_{G^{\mathcal E^+}}O(U)e^{-S_\Lambda(U)}\,d\mu_G^{\mathcal E^+}(U)
+$$
+
+for bounded measurable $O:G^{\mathcal E^+}\to\mathbb C$. The finite-volume Gauss sector is the set of square-integrable functions invariant almost everywhere under all of $G^{\mathcal V}$. For $v\in\mathcal V$ and $X\in\mathfrak g$, let $X^{(v)}$ be the tangent vector at the identity of $G^{\mathcal V}$ with component $X$ at $v$ and zero at other vertices. If $\alpha(h,U)=h\cdot U$, define the infinitesimal **Gauss generator** on $C^\infty(G^{\mathcal E^+})$ by
+
+$$
+(D_{v,X}f)(U):=df_U\!\left(d\alpha_{(\mathbf e,U)}(X^{(v)},0)\right).
+$$
+
+The full Gauss sector is defined by invariance under every vertex gauge transformation; the infinitesimal equations $D_{v,X}f=0$ alone are not asserted to impose invariance under disconnected components of $G$. Link variables and the action are dimensionless. This finite mathematical regulator makes no continuum-limit or empirical claim.
+
+## Definition references
+
+[[def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data|Compact group, Lie algebra, and unitary representation input]]
+
+[[def-countable-choice|Countable-choice assumption carried by the supplied Lie algebra construction]]
+
+[Meyer, §§1.2.3–1.2.4 and §1.4.1.1, printed pp. 11–14 and 22–24](https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73862/1/82.pdf.pdf)
+
+[Lohmayer, §§3.5.2–3.5.5 and §§4.1–4.2, printed pp. 44–51 and 59–63](https://epub.uni-regensburg.de/16031/1/EigenvalueDistributionsOfWilsonLoops.pdf)
