+  def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data: mathematical-premise
+  def-qftx-wilson-loop: mathematical-premise
+  lem-qftx-lattice-data-well-defined: mathematical-premise
+provenance:
+  statement: ai-altered
+  proof: ai-generated
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "H. B. Meyer, Lattice QCD: A Brief Introduction, in Lattice QCD for Nuclear Physics, Lecture Notes in Physics 889"
+      url: "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73862/1/82.pdf.pdf"
+    - title: "Robert Lohmayer, Eigenvalue Distributions of Wilson Loops"
+      url: "https://epub.uni-regensburg.de/16031/1/EigenvalueDistributionsOfWilsonLoops.pdf"
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+proof_strategy: direct
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+For every finite closed edge word $C$ based at $v_0$, every finite-dimensional unitary representation $\rho$, every link configuration $U$, and every vertex gauge transformation $h$, the ordered loop holonomy satisfies $U_C(h\cdot U)=h_{v_0}U_C(U)h_{v_0}^{-1}$. Consequently the normalized Wilson loop is gauge invariant, is unchanged by cyclic rebasing, and is complex conjugated by orientation reversal.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data|Vertex gauge action on link variables]]
+
+[[def-qftx-wilson-loop|Ordered loop holonomy and normalized trace]]
+
+[Meyer, §1.2.3, printed pp. 11–12, on the endpoint transformation law of link transport and trace invariance](https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73862/1/82.pdf.pdf)
+
+[Lohmayer, §3.5.5, printed pp. 48–51, on traced closed lattice holonomy](https://epub.uni-regensburg.de/16031/1/EigenvalueDistributionsOfWilsonLoops.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The finite link gauge action, a based closed edge word $C=(e_1,\ldots,e_\ell)$, and a finite-dimensional unitary representation $\rho$.
+
+[F1] [[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data]]: $(h\cdot U)_e=h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}$ and a face or loop holonomy is the ordered product of its edge variables.
+
+[F2] [[def-qftx-wilson-loop]]: $W_\rho(C;U)=d^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}(\rho(U_{e_1}\cdots U_{e_\ell}))$ for $d\geq1$.
+
+[F3] [[lem-qftx-lattice-data-well-defined]]: the normalized finite-lattice Boltzmann expectation exists and the product measure and action are invariant under every vertex gauge transformation.
+
+## Proof
+
+**Proof technique:** direct.
+
+1.1 Write the vertices of the loop in order as $v_0,v_1,\ldots,v_\ell=v_0$. Substituting [F1] into the ordered product gives
+
+$$
+U_C(h\cdot U)=(h_{v_0}U_{e_1}h_{v_1}^{-1})(h_{v_1}U_{e_2}h_{v_2}^{-1})\cdots(h_{v_{\ell-1}}U_{e_\ell}h_{v_0}^{-1})=h_{v_0}U_C(U)h_{v_0}^{-1},
+$$
+
+because each adjacent factor $h_{v_i}^{-1}h_{v_i}$ is the identity. [given, F1, algebra]
+
+1.2 Applying the homomorphism $\rho$ preserves conjugation, and trace is cyclic, so $\operatorname{Tr}(\rho(h_{v_0})\rho(U_C)\rho(h_{v_0})^{-1})=\operatorname{Tr}(\rho(U_C))$. Thus [F2] is gauge invariant; a cyclic rebase is the same trace after a cyclic permutation of factors. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+1.3 Reversal replaces each edge matrix by its inverse in reverse order, hence replaces $\rho(U_C)$ by its inverse. Since $\rho(U_C)$ is unitary, $\operatorname{Tr}(\rho(U_C)^{-1})=\overline{\operatorname{Tr}(\rho(U_C))}$. Boundedness of the normalized trace and [F3] give a well-defined finite-lattice expectation, unchanged by a gauge transformation of the integration variables. [F2, F3, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
