+---
+id: lem-qftx-lattice-data-well-defined
+kind: lemma
+title: Finite lattice gauge data define an invariant probability
+status: draft
+origin: session
+dependency_level: 2
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+category: quantum-field-theory-mathematics
+axiom_base: "ZF + AC_omega, inherited through the supplied non-Abelian gauge datum; the finite-product argument adds no choice principle."
+deps:
+  - def-countable-choice
+  - def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data
+  - def-differential-of-a-smooth-map
+dependency_roles:
+  def-countable-choice: mathematical-premise
+  def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data: mathematical-premise
+  def-differential-of-a-smooth-map: mathematical-premise
+provenance:
+  statement: ai-altered
+  proof: ai-generated
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: "H. B. Meyer, Lattice QCD: A Brief Introduction, in Lattice QCD for Nuclear Physics, Lecture Notes in Physics 889"
+      url: "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73862/1/82.pdf.pdf"
+    - title: "Robert Lohmayer, Eigenvalue Distributions of Wilson Loops"
+      url: "https://epub.uni-regensburg.de/16031/1/EigenvalueDistributionsOfWilsonLoops.pdf"
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+proof_strategy: direct
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+For every finite oriented lattice two-complex with compact Lie group $G$, normalized bi-invariant link probability $\mu_G$, and continuous conjugation-invariant face actions $\Phi_p$, the vertex formulas $(h\cdot U)_e=h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}$ define a smooth group action on $G^{\mathcal E^+}$. Each face holonomy transforms by conjugation at its base vertex. Consequently the product Haar measure and the Boltzmann weight $e^{-\sum_p\Phi_p(U_p)}$ are gauge invariant, and the partition function satisfies $0<Z_\Lambda<\infty$. Thus the normalized finite-lattice expectation is a well-defined gauge-invariant probability expectation. The infinitesimal Gauss generators defined by differentiating this smooth action are well-defined on smooth functions.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data|Finite oriented lattice variables, gauge action, Haar product, action, and Gauss generators]]
+
+[Meyer, §1.2.4, printed pp. 13–14, on the invariant link measure and finite lattice expectation](https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73862/1/82.pdf.pdf)
+
+[Lohmayer, §4.1, printed pp. 59–60, on gauge invariance and factorization of the pure-gauge measure](https://epub.uni-regensburg.de/16031/1/EigenvalueDistributionsOfWilsonLoops.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, a finite oriented two-complex, a compact Lie group $G$, a normalized bi-invariant probability $\mu_G$, and continuous class functions $\Phi_p$.
+
+[A1] $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is inherited through the supplied construction of $\mathfrak g=T_eG$; the definition does not use arbitrary choice. [[def-countable-choice]]
+
+[F1] [[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data]]: one variable $U_e\in G$ is assigned to each positively oriented edge, reverse edges carry $U_e^{-1}$, vertices act by $h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}$, and face holonomies are ordered products around closed edge words.
+
+[F2] [[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data]]: $G$ is compact and $\mu_G$ is a normalized left- and right-invariant Borel probability; the face actions are continuous and conjugation invariant.
+
+## Proof
+
+**Proof technique:** direct.
+
+1.1 For $h,k\in G^{\mathcal V}$, applying $k$ after $h$ sends an edge $e$ to $k_{s(e)}h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}k_{t(e)}^{-1}=(kh)_{s(e)}U_e(kh)_{t(e)}^{-1}$; the identity tuple acts identically. Hence the formulas define a group action. The action map is smooth because it is built from the smooth group multiplication and inversion on finitely many coordinates, so its differential and each stated Gauss generator exist. [given, F1, algebra]
+
+1.2 Around a face word based at $v_0$, the intermediate factors $h_{v_i}^{-1}h_{v_i}$ cancel, leaving $U_p(h\cdot U)=h_{v_0}U_p(U)h_{v_0}^{-1}$. Thus each $\Phi_p(U_p)$ and the total action are unchanged. On each edge, $U_e\mapsto h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}$ is a left and right translation, so bi-invariance preserves that edge's Haar measure and hence their finite product. [given, F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+1.3 For each face $p$ and each $g\in G$, continuity of $\Phi_p$ gives an open neighborhood on which $|\Phi_p|\leq |\Phi_p(g)|+1$. Compactness of $G$ gives a finite subcover, so each $\Phi_p$ is bounded; summing over the finite face set gives $|S_\Lambda(U)|\leq M$ for some finite $M$. Thus $e^{-M}\leq e^{-S_\Lambda(U)}\leq e^M$, and integration against the product probability gives $e^{-M}\leq Z_\Lambda\leq e^M$. Hence the normalized Boltzmann measure exists and is gauge invariant by step 1.2. [given, F2, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
