---\nid: lem-qftx-planar-plaquette-factorization\nkind: lemma\ntitle: Independent plaquette variables on a finite planar rectangle\nstatus: draft\norigin: session\ndependency_level: 2\ndomain: mathematics\nlibrary: mathematics\ncategory: quantum-field-theory-mathematics\naxiom_base: "ZF + AC_omega, inherited through the finite lattice data supplier; the finite coordinate changes use no additional choice."\ndeps:\n  - def-countable-choice\n  - def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data\ndependency_roles:\n  def-countable-choice: mathematical-premise\n  def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data: mathematical-premise\nprovenance:\n  statement: ai-altered\n  proof: ai-generated\nsources:\n  references:\n    - title: "H. B. Meyer, Lattice QCD: A Brief Introduction, in Lattice QCD for Nuclear Physics, Lecture Notes in Physics 889"\n      url: "https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73862/1/82.pdf.pdf"\n    - title: "Robert Lohmayer, Eigenvalue Distributions of Wilson Loops"\n      url: "https://epub.uni-regensburg.de/16031/1/EigenvalueDistributionsOfWilsonLoops.pdf"\nverification:\n  precheck: pending\nproof_strategy: direct\n---\n\n## Statement\n\nAssume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ and let $G$ be a compact group with normalized bi-invariant Borel probability $\mu$. For positive integers $M,N$, let $\Lambda_{M,N}$ be the finite $M$-by-$N$ square lattice with vertices $(i,j)$, $0\leq i\leq M$, $0\leq j\leq N$, east-oriented horizontal edges $h_{i,j}$, north-oriented vertical edges $v_{i,j}$, and counterclockwise faces $p_{i,j}$, $0\leq i<M$, $0\leq j<N$. Use one independent Haar variable per edge and free boundary edges. Let each face have a positive continuous conjugation-invariant weight $w_{i,j}:G\to(0,\infty)$ with $w_{i,j}(g^{-1})=w_{i,j}(g)$, and normalize the link measure by the product of these weights. For any chosen column $a\in\{0,\ldots,M\}$, there is a unique gauge representative with every horizontal edge and every vertical edge in column $a$ equal to the identity. In that representative, set $P_{i,j}:=V_{i+1,j}V_{i,j}^{-1}$, where $V_{i,j}$ is the vertical link variable after gauge fixing. The plaquette variables $(P_{i,j})$ are independent, and $P_{i,j}$ has probability\n\n$$\nd\nu_{i,j}(g)=\frac{w_{i,j}(g)}{\int_Gw_{i,j}(z)\,d\mu(z)}\,d\mu(g).\n$$\n\nThis is a finite planar lattice statement with no periodic identifications or continuum limit.\n\n## Statement references\n\n[[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data|Finite link configurations, vertex action, face holonomies, and product Haar measure]]\n\n[Lohmayer, §4.1, printed pp. 59–61, on two-dimensional factorization and independent plaquette variables](https://epub.uni-regensburg.de/16031/1/EigenvalueDistributionsOfWilsonLoops.pdf)\n\n[Meyer, §1.4.1.1, printed pp. 22–24, on finite-lattice character and strong-coupling expansions](https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73862/1/82.pdf.pdf)\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, a compact group $G$, a normalized bi-invariant probability $\mu$, the finite rectangular cellulation, and the stated positive class weights.\n\n[A1] $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ is inherited through the supplied Lie-algebra construction in the finite lattice datum; the proof below uses only finite products and deterministic coordinate changes. [[def-countable-choice]]\n\n[F1] [[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data]]: edge variables are $G$-valued, reverse orientations carry inverses, vertices act by left and right multiplication at endpoints, and the link measure is a finite product of $\mu$.\n\n[F2] [[def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data]]: each face holonomy is the ordered product around its oriented boundary, and the action weight is a continuous class function of that holonomy.\n\n## Proof\n\n**Proof technique:** direct change of variables.\n\n1.1 Fix a column $a$. The set $T$ of all horizontal edges together with the vertical edges in column $a$ is a spanning tree: each row is a horizontal path and the selected vertical edges join these row paths in one vertical path, with no other vertical edge in $T$ to close a cycle. Thus $T$ connects every vertex and has no cycle. [given, F1, construct]\n\n1.2 Fix the root $(a,0)$ and set its gauge factor to the identity. Recursively along $T$, each tree-edge condition $h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}=1$ determines the as-yet-unknown gauge factor at the next vertex uniquely, using multiplication and inversion in $G$. Hence every link configuration has a unique root-fixed gauge transform whose tree links are the identity. [given, F1, step 1.1, construct]\n\n1.3 This gauge-fixing change of variables preserves product Haar measure. With root factor $h_{(a,0)}=1$, the inverse coordinate map is $U_e=h_{s(e)}^{-1}h_{t(e)}$ on $T$ and $U_e=h_{s(e)}^{-1}V_eh_{t(e)}$ off $T$. Recovering the $h_v$ recursively along $T$ is a sequence of left or right translations and inversions; for fixed $h$, recovering each off-tree $U_e$ from $V_e$ is also a left and right translation. Bi-invariance therefore preserves each factor in the finite product, so the change of variables sends $\prod_e d\mu(U_e)$ to $\prod_{v\ne(a,0)}d\mu(h_v)\prod_{e\notin T}d\mu(V_e)$. Integrating the tree coordinates contributes $1$. [given, F1, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n1.4 In tree gauge all horizontal variables are $1$ and $V_{a,j}=1$. The counterclockwise face word gives $P_{i,j}=V_{i+1,j}V_{i,j}^{-1}$. For a fixed row $j$, starting at $V_{a,j}=1$, the equations determine $V_{i+1,j}=P_{i,j}V_{i,j}$ to the right of $a$ and $V_{i,j}=P_{i,j}^{-1}V_{i+1,j}$ to its left. Thus the map from the non-tree vertical variables in row $j$ to its $M$ plaquette variables is bijective and triangular; each inverse-coordinate change is a left or right translate and preserves product Haar measure. The rows use disjoint vertical variables, so the full plaquette map is a product measure-preserving bijection. [given, F1, F2, step 1.3, algebra]\n\n1.5 In these variables the unnormalized density is exactly $\prod_{i,j}w_{i,j}(P_{i,j})$. Each positive continuous $w_{i,j}$ is bounded above and below by positive constants: continuity gives a bounded neighborhood at each group element, and compactness gives a finite subcover; applying the same argument to $1/w_{i,j}$ gives a positive lower bound. Thus each $z_{i,j}=\int_Gw_{i,j}\,d\mu$ is finite and strictly positive. The product Haar measure factors by step 1.4, so the partition function is $\prod_{i,j}z_{i,j}$; dividing by it yields the product of the displayed one-plaquette probabilities. [given, F1, F2, step 1.4, algebra] ∎\n
