items/def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction.md:45:**(b) Tetrad at an event and tetrad field.** A **tetrad at $p\in A$** is an ordered basis $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of $V$ with $\eta(e_a,e_b)=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)_{ab}$ and $e_0$ future, $e_0\in C^+$ ([[def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors]], [[def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets]]): this is the basis of an inertial frame without an origin and without the orientation condition. A **tetrad field** on a nonempty open subset $\Omega\subseteq A$ is a tuple $(E_0,E_1,E_2,E_3)$ of smooth vector fields on $\Omega$ ([[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]], type $(1,0)$) whose values at every $p\in\Omega$ form a tetrad at $p$. A tetrad field is **observer-adapted** to a prescribed congruence $n$ when $E_0=n$. Along a prescribed $C^1$ future timelike worldline $x:I\to A$, observer adaptation means $E_0(x(s))=x'(s)/\sqrt{-\eta(x'(s),x'(s))}$ whenever $x(s)\in\Omega$; this is its normalized future unit tangent ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]).
items/def-sr-chart-tetrad-and-congruence-distinction.md:47:**(c) Observer congruence.** An **observer congruence** on a nonempty open subset $\Omega\subseteq A$ is a smooth vector field $n:\Omega\to V$ with $\eta(n,n)=-1$ and $n(p)\in C^+$ for every $p\in\Omega$. Its **observers** are its maximal integral curves ([[def-integral-curve-of-a-vector-field]], [[thm-unique-maximal-integral-curve-through-each-point]]). Each observer is a future timelike worldline ([[def-sr-regular-causal-curve-and-worldline]]): its tangent at a parameter value $s$ is $n(x(s))$, which is future timelike, and it is regular because $\eta(n,n)=-1$ makes $n$ nowhere zero. **Completeness** of the integral curves, a global product structure of $\Omega$, and global synchronization are not asserted by this definition.
items/pthm-rpm-prescribed-force-local-motion.md:47:Let $(M,g,N)$ be a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold and let $m,c>0$. Let $\mathcal V\subset TM$ be an open set of future timelike vectors, and let $f$ be a smooth vector field along the bundle projection $\mathcal V\to M$, with $f(p,V)\in T_pM$. For the named massive test body, adopt the constant-mass force model of [[post-rpm-prescribed-force-law]]: $P=mU$, $g(U,U)=-c^2$, $D_\tau P=f(x,U)$, and $g(U,f(x,U))=0$. If $\tau$ is measured in seconds, then $P$ has SI units kilogram metres per second and $f$ has units newtons.
