items/thm-emg-xs-kn-verification.md:119:1.2 Put $w=\sqrt{|\det g|}$. For any smooth vector field $J$, the vector-current density identity in [F3] and the coordinate formula $\nabla_iJ^i=\partial_iJ^i+\Gamma^i{}_{ik}J^k$ imply $\Gamma^i{}_{ik}=w^{-1}\partial_kw$ by comparing arbitrary $J$. For the antisymmetric field, the tensor derivative expands as $\nabla_i\mathcal F^{ij}=\partial_i\mathcal F^{ij}+\Gamma^i{}_{ik}\mathcal F^{kj}+\Gamma^j{}_{ik}\mathcal F^{ik}$. The last term vanishes by symmetry of the connection in $i,k$ and antisymmetry of the field. Thus $\nabla_i\mathcal F^{ij}=w^{-1}\partial_i(w\mathcal F^{ij})$. In the rational chart $(x^0,r,y=\cos\theta,\phi)$, [F2] gives $w=\Sigma$, so its four zero Maxwell-density residuals give $v^j:=\nabla_i\mathcal F^{ij}=0$ there. To relate this divergence to $d(\star_g\mathcal F)$ without assuming the divergence equation, fix any pole-free point and use the point-normal chart supplied by the direct prerequisite [[thm-gr-point-normal-and-exponential-coordinate-charts]], choosing its orientation positive (if necessary, reverse one spatial coordinate, which preserves the normal form). At the point, $g_{ab}=\eta_{ab}$, $\Gamma^a{}_{bc}=0$, and $\partial g_{ab}=0$. Differentiating $g^{ac}g_{cb}=\delta^a_b$ gives $\partial g^{ab}=0$ there, and $w=\sqrt{|\det(g_{ab})|}$ gives $\partial w=0$. Put $E_i:=-\mathcal F_{0i}$ and $B_i:=\tfrac12\epsilon_{ijk}\mathcal F_{jk}$ as local component abbreviations only, with $\epsilon_{123}=+1$, and define $(\operatorname{curl}B)_k:=\epsilon_{kij}\partial_iB_j$. The Hodge component formula in [F3] gives $\mathcal F^{0i}=E_i$, $\mathcal F^{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, $({\star_g\mathcal F})_{0i}=B_i$, and $({\star_g\mathcal F})_{ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}E_k$ at the point. Since the inverse-metric and volume derivatives vanish there, differentiating the Hodge formula contributes no extra terms. The coordinate formulas for divergence and exterior derivative therefore give $v^0=-\partial_iE_i$, $v^k=\partial_0E_k-(\operatorname{curl}B)_k$, $d({\star_g\mathcal F})_{123}=\partial_iE_i$, and $d({\star_g\mathcal F})_{0ij}=\epsilon_{ijk}(\partial_0E_k-(\operatorname{curl}B)_k)$. Also $(\iota_v\mathrm{vol}_g)_{123}=v^0$ and $(\iota_v\mathrm{vol}_g)_{0ij}=-\epsilon_{ijk}v^k$. Hence $d({\star_g\mathcal F})=-\iota_v\mathrm{vol}_g$ at this point, and therefore at every pole-free point. As $v=0$, $d({\star_g\mathcal F})=0$ on that region. In the full-sphere extension, this three-form is smooth by [F5] and vanishes on the dense pole-free set, so it vanishes at the poles by continuity. [F3, F5, A1, A2, algebra]
