items/thm-qftx-scalar-eg-induction.md:169:9.1 Apply [F9] to this module and cocycle to obtain $v\in V$ with $c(X)=\rho(X)v$. Thus $t^1-v$ has zero infinitesimal combined defect, retains the engineering bounds, and agrees with $t^0$ off the diagonal. Its combined orbit is constant along each one-parameter subgroup by the preceding smooth test-pairing argument. Along a smooth group path $g(s)$ the orbit derivative is the transported infinitesimal action of $g(s)^{-1}g'(s)$ and hence is zero. In the identity component every point is reachable by a piecewise smooth path: the set reachable from the identity and its complement are open, using straight paths in coordinate balls. Connectedness therefore gives full $SO^+(1,3)$ covariance. Work separately in homogeneous balanced-label degrees, whose finite spaces are Lorentz invariant, and recover total-derivative descendants by [F11]; these specify one corrected numerical map $\nu_n^2$ on their algebraic direct sum. Use $T_n^2=\mathcal R(\nu_n^2)$ for every field degree. Reconstruction commutes with Poincare transformations, since $\tau_{\gamma\cdot\phi}(\gamma\cdot F)=\gamma\cdot(\tau_\phi F)$ and the numerical family is equivariant; step 7.1 proves its field independence and representation independence. In particular a correction chosen on a numerical label is reused in all occurrences of that label as a sub-polynomial, never chosen again for each spectator-field degree. The choices of these finite-block corrections are permitted by [A1]. Translation covariance is retained because every correction is a relative delta jet lifted by the same center-coordinate integral as in step 6.1. [A1, F1, F8, F9, F11, step 6.1, step 7.1, step 7.2, step 8.1, algebra]
items/thm-fd-periodic-pressure-recovery.md:47:Let $Q_d=\mathbb R^d/(2\pi\mathbb Z)^d$ have normalized measure $d\mu=(2\pi)^{-d}dx$. For $\alpha=(\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_d)\in\mathbb N_0^d$, write $|\alpha|=\sum_j\alpha_j$ and $\partial^\alpha=\partial_1^{\alpha_1}\cdots\partial_d^{\alpha_d}$. Give $C^\infty(Q_d;\mathbb R)$ the Fr\'echet topology generated by $p_m(\phi)=\max_{|\alpha|\le m}\|\partial^\alpha\phi\|_\infty$, for $m\in\mathbb N_0$, where the derivatives are those of the smooth periodic lift to $\mathbb R^d$. A periodic distribution is a continuous real-linear functional on this test space, extended complex-linearly to complex-valued tests. For such a distribution $S$, define $\widehat S(k)=\langle S,e^{-ik\cdot x}\rangle$ for $e_k(x)=e^{ik\cdot x}$ and define its mean to be $\widehat S(0)=\langle S,1\rangle$. For an integrable function $g$, the pairing is $\langle g,\phi\rangle=\int_{Q_d}g\phi\,d\mu$. Define distributional derivatives by $\langle\partial_jS,\phi\rangle=-\langle S,\partial_j\phi\rangle$. For a smooth scalar test, $\nabla\phi=(\partial_1\phi,\ldots,\partial_d\phi)$ and $\Delta\phi=\sum_j\partial_j^2\phi$; for a smooth vector field $v$, $\operatorname{div}v=\sum_j\partial_jv_j$, $(v\otimes v)_{ij}=v_iv_j$, and $(v\cdot\nabla)v_i=\sum_jv_j\partial_jv_i$. In particular, $\operatorname{div}u=0$ distributionally means $-\int_{Q_d}u\cdot\nabla\phi\,d\mu=0$ for every real test function $\phi$. Equality of periodic distributions means equality on every such test function. Assume Countable Choice. Let $d\in\{1,2,3\}$, let $s>d/2+1$, and let $u\in H^s(Q_d;\mathbb R^d)$ satisfy $\operatorname{div}u=0$ in this sense. Then $u\otimes u\in H^s$, the mean-zero scalar $\pi=Q(u\otimes u)$ belongs to $H^s$, and in $H^{s-1}$,
