items/lem-abbv-fixed-component-euler-invertibility.md:66:1.3 If $\nu_j$ has nonzero weights, choose $X\in\mathfrak t$ outside the finite union of their kernels. On a real $\chi$-weight subbundle, the infinitesimal action $A_X$ satisfies $A_X^2=-d\chi(X)^2 I$, so $J_\chi=A_X/d\chi(X)$ is an invariant complex structure; their direct sum gives one on $\nu_j$. Its complex orientation may agree or disagree with the chosen normal orientation, with comparison sign $\epsilon_j$. The weight projections vary continuously, so their ranks, and therefore the character multiplicities, are constant on connected $F_j$. If there are no weights, then $\nu_j=0$ and the complex structure is vacuous. [given, A1, A2, step 1.2, construct]
items/def-tgr-local-fluid-state.md:47:Let $O\subseteq M$ be a nonempty open set and let $u$ be an observer congruence on $O$ in the sense of [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]]: $u$ is a smooth vector field whose values are future-directed unit timelike observers, so $g(u,u)=-1$. Its maximal integral curves have open interval parameter domains and are smooth regular future timelike curves; no completeness, geodesic equation, or orthogonal rest-space foliation is assumed. The frame in this definition means this congruence field and its associated pointwise rest spaces. It is not an adapted chart or a tetrad field; those are distinct objects in [[def-gr-observer-congruence-and-adapted-charts]] and [[def-gr-local-frame-and-tetrad-fields]]. The physical four-velocity associated with $u$ is $U=cu$, with $g(U,U)=-c^2$.
