+
+## Definition
+
+Assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ and take finite oriented lattice data with compact group $G$, link variables $U_e$, and vertex action $U_e\mapsto h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}$. Fix a positive-dimensional finite-dimensional complex representation $\rho:G\to U(W,h)$, with $d=\dim_{mathbb C}W\geq1$. A **based closed lattice loop** is a finite edge word $C=(e_1,\ldots,e_\ell)$ whose consecutive endpoints match and whose final endpoint is its starting vertex $v_0$. Its ordered holonomy is $U_C:=U_{e_1}\cdots U_{e_\ell}$, in traversal order with the first edge on the left. The **normalized Wilson loop** is
+
+$$
+W_\rho(C;U):=\frac{1}{d}\operatorname{Tr}_W\!\big(\rho(U_C)\big).
+$$
+
+The loop is dimensionless. Changing the base vertex by a cyclic rebase conjugates $U_C$ and leaves $W_\rho$ unchanged; reversing the loop gives $W_\rho(C^{-1};U)=\overline{W_\rho(C;U)}$. On a fixed finite lattice with a specified finite product Haar probability and bounded action, $\langle W_\rho(C)\rangle_\Lambda$ is the ordinary normalized finite-dimensional integral. No renormalized continuum Wilson loop or continuum limit is defined here.
+
+## Definition references
