## Scope and plan reconciliation

Authored the assigned pair only: A `qftx-nonabelian-lattice-mathematics` (order 962) and B `qftx-nonabelian-lattice-mathematics-examples`. Both page carriers contain YAML frontmatter only; A contains eight mathematical items and B contains two worked examples. The A page still requires `qftx-gauge-geometry`; B still requires A. The page item cap is not approached.

The active `plan-spec.json` page IDs, title, category, companion, order, and A-page prerequisite agree with the task and design at `research/plan-advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry-track.md` L79. The design/prose scaffold left `def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data` with `proposed_deps: []`, but that definition uses the exact compact Lie group, Lie algebra, and specified smooth unitary representation from `def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data`. Its direct mathematical dependency is therefore declared rather than leaving an unsupported interface assumption. This makes its level 1 because its in-run supplier is batch 2, level 0. The owner direction requires substantial B examples although the plan placeholders had empty inventories; the two examples below are authorized inventory expansion. No physical postulate or physical result was added: all claims are mathematical and finite-regulator only.

## Authored claims, conventions, and dependencies

All dependencies below are mathematical. Levels count only in-run item suppliers, using zero for no in-run suppliers and one plus the maximum in-run supplier level otherwise.

| Item | Claim / role | Direct dependencies | Level |
|---|---|---|---:|
| `def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data` | Finite oriented 2-complex, G-valued links, vertex action, face holonomy, product bi-invariant probability, bounded-action expectation, Gauss sector and infinitesimal generator | `def-countable-choice`, `def-qftx-nonabelian-gauge-data`, `def-differential-of-a-smooth-map` | 1 |
| `lem-qftx-lattice-data-well-defined` | Smooth action; conjugated face holonomy; invariant product and Boltzmann probability; positive finite partition function; closed L2 Gauss sector and defined directional generators | `def-countable-choice`, `def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data`, `def-differential-of-a-smooth-map` | 2 |
| `def-qftx-wilson-loop` | Based ordered holonomy and normalized unitary trace | `def-countable-choice`, `def-finite-dimensional-representation-of-a-group-over-a-field`, `def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data` | 2 |
| `lem-qftx-wilson-loop-gauge-invariance` | Endpoint factors cancel; trace is invariant under gauge action/rebase and conjugates on reversal | `def-countable-choice`, `def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data`, `def-qftx-wilson-loop` | 3 |
| `def-qftx-su2-wilson-plaquette-measure` | Explicit SU(2) matrix coordinates, normalized round measure and dimensionless Wilson plaquette law | `def-countable-choice`, `def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data`, `ex-unitary-and-special-unitary-lie-groups`, `thm-heine-borel-rn` | 2 |
| `lem-qftx-planar-plaquette-factorization` | Root-fixed spanning-tree gauge and product-Haar-preserving plaquette coordinates on a finite free-boundary rectangle | `def-countable-choice`, `def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data` | 2 |
| `lem-qftx-su2-one-plaquette-average` | Exact SU(2) matrix expectation, strict 0<lambda<1 and lambda=beta/4+O(beta^3) | `def-countable-choice`, `def-qftx-su2-wilson-plaquette-measure`, `ex-unitary-and-special-unitary-lie-groups`, `thm-heine-borel-rn` | 3 |
| `thm-qftx-strong-coupling-wilson-area-law` | Exact finite free-boundary 2D SU(2) rectangular loop value lambda^A for 0<beta<=1 | `def-countable-choice`, `def-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-gauge-data`, `def-qftx-wilson-loop`, `def-qftx-su2-wilson-plaquette-measure`, `lem-qftx-planar-plaquette-factorization`, `lem-qftx-su2-one-plaquette-average` | 4 |
| `ex-qftx-su2-one-plaquette-wilson-average` | One-face partition factor and Wilson expectation, including the small-beta expansion | `def-countable-choice`, `def-qftx-wilson-loop`, `def-qftx-su2-wilson-plaquette-measure`, `lem-qftx-planar-plaquette-factorization`, `lem-qftx-su2-one-plaquette-average` | 4 |
| `ex-qftx-su2-two-plaquette-wilson-loop` | Area-two specialization and beta^2/16+O(beta^4) expansion | `def-countable-choice`, `lem-qftx-su2-one-plaquette-average`, `thm-qftx-strong-coupling-wilson-area-law` | 5 |

The oriented-link convention is $(h\cdot U)_e=h_{s(e)}U_eh_{t(e)}^{-1}$; reverse links carry inverses; face and loop products are multiplied in traversal order with the first link on the left. The SU(2) Wilson weight is $e^{-S_\beta}$ for $S_\beta=\beta\sum_p(1-\tfrac12\operatorname{Tr}U_p)$ and $\beta=4/g_0^2$. The exact area result is for a finite 2D square container with free boundary and a counterclockwise rectangular loop from its lower-left corner; it makes no infinite-volume, continuum, mass-gap, or empirical claim. The strong-coupling window is stated explicitly as $0<\beta\leq1$ (equivalently $g_0^2\geq4$).

The full Gauss sector is defined in $L^2$ of the normalized finite-lattice probability by invariance of each equivalence class under every vertex gauge transformation. Its closedness follows from the intersection of the fixed spaces of the unitary pullback operators; only directional derivatives on smooth functions are asserted for the infinitesimal generators. No connected-component claim is inferred from the infinitesimal equations.

## Source review and retrieval history

Two independent full texts were completely inspected over the cited sections, fetched and stamped:

1. H. B. Meyer, “Lattice QCD: A Brief Introduction,” *Lattice QCD for Nuclear Physics*, Lecture Notes in Physics 889 (2015), direct PDF `https://ndl.ethernet.edu.et/bitstream/123456789/73862/1/82.pdf.pdf`. The containing PDF has 255 pages. Read §1.2.3–§1.2.4, printed pp. 11–14 (link transport, endpoint transformation, closed-loop trace, plaquette and Wilson action) and §1.4.1.1, printed pp. 22–24 (Haar-link integrals and leading strong-coupling tiling). Meyer’s area term is a leading-order four-dimensional expansion and is contextual; it is not used as proof of the exact finite two-dimensional claim.
2. Robert Lohmayer, *Eigenvalue Distributions of Wilson Loops*, dissertation, Universität Regensburg (2010), direct PDF `https://epub.uni-regensburg.de/16031/1/EigenvalueDistributionsOfWilsonLoops.pdf`. The PDF has 213 pages. Read §§3.5.2–3.5.5, printed pp. 44–51, and §§4.1–4.2.2, pp. 59–64. These give the lattice Wilson-loop conventions, two-dimensional partition-function factorization, rectangular area dependence and non-Abelian one-plaquette matrix integral. The thesis uses an infinite-lattice temporal gauge; the item proof replaces it with a root-fixed finite spanning-tree gauge. Its Gross–Witten discussion is not imported into the SU(2) result.

The source harvest records these treatments’ supported items and local-proof limitations in `batch-6.coverage.json`. No source item is treated as a proof supplier. The previous Negele–Grießhammer–Lehmann QCD lecture-book selection remains preserved as historical retrieval context: source-fetch-check fetched and stamped the full PDF, but a separate local reading download timed out. It is not counted as one of the two fully read treatments. Search queries run during source resolution were `"Lattice QCD: A Brief Introduction" Meyer Lecture Notes in Physics 889 PDF` and `"Eigenvalue Distributions of Wilson Loops" Lohmayer PDF`; both selected alternatives were confirmed at their direct full-text repositories. The alternative full-text sources were read successfully, so no active source was dropped and no unresolved source proof gap remains.

